#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Lab 9 B4's evaluation: the SDN analyst without tools against the analyst with the trace tool,
on 19.13's fixed snapshots and the lab hardware's kept ones (docs/HEAL_TO_INTENT.md 19.13;
modules/sdn/b4_eval.py).

    # on the host, the deployed copy run as a file (it adopts the app's interpreter): first
    # what it would spend, asking nothing
    nmas-host nmas -- <checkout>/scripts/nmas-sdn-b4-eval --list <network> --runs 10 \\
        --out /tmp/b4-eval
    # then the run, a person's explicit decision to spend
    nmas-host nmas -- <checkout>/scripts/nmas-sdn-b4-eval --list <network> --runs 10 \\
        --out /tmp/b4-eval --yes-spend

**The lab hardware's snapshots** (C2965): ``--snapshots <folder>`` names a copy of the
network's ``sdn_analyst/`` folder (its ``snapshots/`` and ``planted.json``, as SDN › Flows'
Collect now and the planted-issue sheet kept them on the lab's switches), outside the checkout
and only read; ``--clean <id,id>`` names the snapshots kept with every planted issue restored
(the sheet left as it is). Each snapshot whose seal holds is a case of the role ``hardware``,
its planted issues the sheet's planted at or before its collection, each found by B1's own
rule. An issue removed from the sheet is a correction (B1's meaning, C2913) and counts for no
snapshot, whenever it was removed. A clean one is benign; one with issues still standing on the
sheet is said so in the report and scored benign. A snapshot whose seal fails, or that holds no
standing issue and is not named clean, is skipped and said. ``--roles hardware`` runs them
alone; with --snapshots and no --roles, they run beside 19.13's roles. 19.13's pass rule stays
on the blind role alone.

    nmas-host nmas -- <checkout>/scripts/nmas-sdn-b4-eval --list <network> --runs 10 \\
        --roles hardware --snapshots /tmp/b4-snapshots --clean snap-<id> --out /tmp/b4-eval

**The Lab 9 sitting's cases** (the role ``sitting``, run by default beside 19.13's; never
blind, and no part of the pass rule): what the analyst got wrong on the lab's hardware on
2026-11-10, modelled on the test fabric (tests/fixtures/ai_cases/sdn_analyse/lab9_sitting.json):
a planted flow whose output port leads away from its destination, to be found, and a drop of
DHCP replies beside a forward of DHCP requests, whose matches are disjoint, which no finding
above info may cite together; each in IPv4 and IPv6. ``--roles sitting`` runs them alone.

**A candidate prompt** (``--prompt N``): both arms ask version N of the analyst's prompt, the
trace tool the two built on it (modules/ai/sdn_analyst_tools.py `PASSES_OF`), in place of the
ones in use, so a new version is measured before it is adopted (docs/TESTING.md, "AI prompt
versions"); the first line printed and the report name the prompts asked. Version 2 says an
evidence item is one id and nothing else. Without it, the versions in use are asked.

    nmas-host nmas -- <checkout>/scripts/nmas-sdn-b4-eval --list <network> --runs 10 \\
        --prompt 2 --out /tmp/b4-eval-v2

**It spends only with --yes-spend.** Without it, it prints the cases, the most calls and about
what they cost (each arm's own over-estimate of each snapshot), and stops with nothing asked
(exit 2). Never run in CI: its tests run it on a fake provider.

**Recorded as an evaluation, never a person** (C1846): the actor is ``evaluation:<run>`` (none
given is ``evaluation:sdn-b4-eval-<UTC time>``), the trigger the evaluation's, so the spend
counts against the caps under its run. Each case's snapshot is asked of the baseline
(`analyst.ask_snapshot`, B1's one call) and the technique (`sdn_analyst_tools.ask_snapshot`, the
read loop, at most two calls), in turn, --runs times; neither writes the network's analyst
store, so the network's page is unchanged. A refusal before anything is sent (the topology
class off, hosted AI off, a cap) stops the run there (exit 3).

**The report** (--out, never inside the checkout): ``b4-eval-<run>.md`` for the lab document and
``b4-eval-<run>.json`` for B5's cost log, each technique's findings, misses, false positives,
benign runs answered as a threat, cases reliable with McNemar's exact test and a bootstrap
interval, calls, the recorded cost and the time to an answer, by role (recorded, held out,
blind, hardware), and 19.13's pass rule on the blind role. Each run that missed, kept a false
positive or answered a benign case as a threat is also kept in the network's record of
evaluation runs (C2943, modules/sdn/evaluations.py), where History › Export's failure log lists
it.
"""
import os
import sys

sys.dont_write_bytecode = True
ROOT = os.path.dirname(os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)))
sys.path.insert(0, ROOT)
# Phase 4 section 8.1: the interpreter flask-app runs, before any other import.
from modules.app_interpreter import adopt  # noqa: E402
adopt(__name__)


def main(argv=None) -> int:
    from modules.ai import sdn_analyst_tools
    from modules.sdn import analyst, b4_eval

    os.chdir(ROOT)
    return b4_eval.main(sys.argv[1:] if argv is None else argv, root=ROOT,
                        arms={"baseline": analyst.ask_snapshot,
                              "tools": sdn_analyst_tools.ask_snapshot})


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
